00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:12.236
Hello everyone. My name is Vishesh. As Sid mentioned.

00:00:12.916 --> 00:00:16.756
And if you are wondering, I didn't wear this jacket on the MRT.

00:00:17.916 --> 00:00:26.316
It just makes me feel like a weirdo. But thankfully this is the place
where I can be wearing this jacket and for everyone to understand.

00:00:27.316 --> 00:00:35.676
So today we are going to talk about how do we take POCs from
a demo concept which everyone likes, to a production deployment,

00:00:36.156 --> 00:00:39.356
and how we can solve the Agentic AI's toughest challenges.

00:00:39.356 --> 00:00:41.356
While doing that,

00:00:42.116 --> 00:00:47.716
we have the agenda, first of all, when to build, to build, to build a new thing,

00:00:47.716 --> 00:00:52.276
or to use something which is existing and then
figure out the non-functional requirements.

00:00:52.276 --> 00:01:01.196
So operational excellence, data, trust, and reliability are the non-functional
requirements which organizations generally miss in the POC stage.

00:01:02.396 --> 00:01:06.236
This is the key metric that we want to start to understand.

00:01:06.876 --> 00:01:12.036
We have a great demo that worked fine, and between the demo for an organization,

00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:19.956
what we have found as a as a survey is that 46% of those use cases,
those POCs don't make it to production.

00:01:19.956 --> 00:01:23.956
And it is absolutely okay for some of them to not make it to production.

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:31.036
That's good. But 46% of them not making it
to production is a challenge that we want to solve for.

00:01:31.356 --> 00:01:40.436
There might be real business use cases which have real money value
for the business, and if they don't make it to production, that's a loss.

00:01:42.876 --> 00:01:49.916
When I. So let's take this example.
We are starting with an application or an idea which we built.

00:01:49.916 --> 00:01:52.476
It works on our machine. Absolutely fine.

00:01:52.516 --> 00:01:58.036
A single user works great. Does it go to. Is it good enough to go to production.

00:01:58.276 --> 00:02:00.956
So functionally it works. The workflow works.

00:02:00.956 --> 00:02:09.196
The orchestration works on one machine.
But does it really have it in itself by the builders of that POC?

00:02:09.716 --> 00:02:11.916
Have they considered the non-functional requirements?

00:02:11.916 --> 00:02:14.116
And these four are the non-functional pillars.

00:02:14.116 --> 00:02:16.996
So operational excellence being grounded in data,

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:24.236
having the trust and the reliability of the of the entire
solution is essentially the non-functional requirement that we need

00:02:24.236 --> 00:02:28.796
to have a close eye on, even in the in the POC stage.

00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:36.036
This is not something that we need to consider when we go to production,
because it's already too late by then.

00:02:36.076 --> 00:02:39.596
We have to focus on these things in the POC stage itself.

00:02:41.276 --> 00:02:47.036
So the first point is do you need to build
custom or can you just start with what you have?

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:55.916
So if you have a use case which is related to employee
productivity or coding assistant or customer Summer experience.

00:02:55.916 --> 00:02:58.516
We already have built solutions for that.

00:02:59.316 --> 00:03:05.516
They are ready to scale. They can be deployed
in days to weeks time and you don't have to build for it.

00:03:06.876 --> 00:03:11.236
For example, we have quick. We have Kiro and Amazon Connect for these use cases.

00:03:11.836 --> 00:03:16.076
If you have a security or a DevOps use case, we have frontier agents for that.

00:03:16.516 --> 00:03:20.996
But what if you don't have these particular use cases?

00:03:20.996 --> 00:03:28.796
You now need to build custom. And for example,
if you have a unique business logic which is very specific to your organization,

00:03:28.796 --> 00:03:36.636
and that needs to be part of the solution, or if you have to integrate with,
let's say, a mainframe from the 90s within your application,

00:03:36.636 --> 00:03:39.076
that's a custom integration that's required.

00:03:39.076 --> 00:03:44.236
Or if you have your agent, let’s say, needs to
speak the language of your business,

00:03:44.236 --> 00:03:49.396
for example, Basel three in banking or ICD ten codes in healthcare.

00:03:49.396 --> 00:03:56.796
If those are your use cases, then you need to build custom.

00:03:56.796 --> 00:04:00.116
This is probably my favorite slide from the session.

00:04:00.116 --> 00:04:03.436
If you see in blue, we have. AgentCore. Amazon Bedrock.

00:04:03.436 --> 00:04:14.036
AgentCore. And underneath AgentCore itself, we have multiple capabilities
that AgentCore provides, which allows us to build custom Agentic solutions.

00:04:14.356 --> 00:04:21.356
This is the example of the undifferentiated
heavy lifting that AWS can do on your behalf.

00:04:21.676 --> 00:04:26.116
Your job in the business that you are in is to solve for business problems.

00:04:26.396 --> 00:04:31.476
These are infrastructure operational issues which you can offload to AWS.

00:04:31.716 --> 00:04:37.676
While AWS can do a great job in making these available
for your use cases and scaling them as required.

00:04:38.556 --> 00:04:45.076
We'll go over them as we go along. So let's talk about operational excellence,
which is the first non-functional pillar.

00:04:45.156 --> 00:04:52.836
From that we should consider the question to be asked is can you
really operate this in production at the scale you need.

00:04:52.876 --> 00:05:00.000
The question is important because what may work for one user may still work
for ten users, but does it work for 100,000 users?

